	)
	(text "Similarly to Power Supply, the motor has is owm connector because \nit demands high currents (up to 4.5A spikes).\nThis connector does not need interlock because if it not properly \r\nsecured, the motor will not receive power supply, entering the \r\nfail safe state of motor coasting"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 291.084 60.96 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "44e4a450-588c-4c2f-b97d-3e1656b037b8")
	)
	(text "Microcontroller"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 90.424 147.32 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "4d1b96e1-3432-487b-88dc-bfacf5a4e183")
	)
	(text "Motor Position"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 42.164 53.34 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "4f8bd7e0-81e8-4628-889d-f9ec80b23450")
	)
	(text "Power Supply has its owm connector because it has higher current\ndemands (up to 4.5A spikes).\nThis connector does not need interlock because if it not properly \nsecured, the motor will not receive power supply, entering the \nfail safe state of motor coasting"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 290.83 38.608 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "580586eb-7074-4c6a-ae17-107ef641ab6b")
	)
	(text "From LED datasheet the Typical Voltage drop at 5 mA is 2.95V.\nKnowing this, I can compute the series resistor:\n	R = (3.3V - V_forw_LED) / 5 mA = 70 Ohm\n	--> E12 series (5% Toll.), R = 82 Ohm\n\nIf the Actual Forward Voltage of the LED is higher, it will simply light up with\nlower luminosity, so using the typical Forward Voltage value is ok (I don't need to \nbe precise on the actual current flowing in the LED, I just need it to light up)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 216.408 212.344 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "5f914a0b-67fd-450a-8a04-89385e576795")
	)
	(text "Connectors"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 336.042 16.002 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "619cd2a5-f0d9-44f0-b7ae-5e8b615653e8")
	)
	(text "Power Supply"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 249.682 108.966 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "776be629-8a5f-4e5a-8142-85213e4860cf")
	)
	(text "The vehicle speed is simulated using a manually controlled 20 kOhm potentiometer. Since the input is changed\nby the user, its useful bandwidth is very low. An RC low-pass filter is added to reduce noise from the\npotentiometer wiper, while maintaining a response time that appears instantaneous to the user (<50 ms).\n\nA maximum settling time of 30 ms is chosen, giving sufficient margin to the ADC and uC \npost-processing to recive the information in respect to the 50 ms limit.\n\nThe potentiometer output impedance must be considered, as it contributes to the filter time constant:\n    R_TH,max = R_POT / 4 = 20k / 4 = 5 kOhm\n    tau_max = (R_TH,max + R_F) * C_F\n    T_settle = 5 * tau_max\n\nWith R_F = 1 kOhm and C_F = 1 uF:\n    tau_max = (5k + 1k) * 1u = 6 ms\n    T_settle,max = 5 * 6 ms = 30 ms\n\nA first-order filter is considered sufficient, since significant out-of-band signal components are not\nexpected. The actual noise rejection and response time will be verified experimentally."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 90.424 32.004 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "77da6792-d0e5-4afa-b04e-c0e295856906")
	)
	(text "Interlock End"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 370.078 76.454 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "7a02d7df-69e9-412c-9b19-429567ca89ee")
	)
	(text "The bus uses split termination with two 60.4 Ohm resistors and a 4.7nF capacitor \nconnected from their midpoint to GND. The two resistors provide approximately \n120 Ohm differential termination, while the capacitor creates a low-pass filtering\neffect for common-mode noise. \nDuring normal CAN communication, CANH and CANL move in opposite directions, so the \nmidpoint voltage remains approximately constant and the capacitor has\nlittle effect on the differential signal.\r\n\nA larger capacitor would provide stronger common-mode filtering, but could also \nintroduce problems under the fault situation when when one CAN line becomes \ndisconnected. In this case, the differential symmetry is lost and the capacitor \ncan affect the useful signal, introducing an RC delay that may prevent the signal \nfrom reaching the required voltage thresholds before the CAN sampling point. \nFor this reason, 4.7nF was selected instead of a much larger value, such as \n100nF, to obtain some common-mode filtering without unnecessarily \ndegrading signal integrity under fault conditions.\r\n\r\nThe termination resistors are sized considering possible short circuit fault to \nthe 12V main supply of one of the CAN lines, which should not result in fathal malfunctions.\n	P_MAX = (12V / (2*60.4))^2 * 60.4 = 0.596 W \n	--> Max power loss on each resitor\n	-->	Termination resistor should at least be rated for 1 W (and PCB should\n		be designed to handle the heat dissipation)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 222.25 34.29 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "7d499f8d-f462-4eba-9a09-beaf7c21790e")
	)
	(text "CAN"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 222.504 17.018 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "81844af8-c1a0-4382-9786-15700af297fd")
	)
	(text "No common-mode choke is used, since no dedicated EMC testing is\nplanned to calibrate its parameters, guaranteeing an actual \nimprovement of the CAN performance."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 173.99 22.606 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "82bb3d17-0cf0-47f0-8cd3-79c2a83ac905")
	)
	(text "Power Supply Interface"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 312.674 21.59 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "8382b6cd-631d-47a2-bc08-e5748d021ade")
	)
	(text "The chosen CAN transceiver uses a 5V supply for the bus interface \r\nand a 3.3V VIO supply for compatibility with the MCU logic levels.\r\nThe 5V supply provides more voltage headroom for the CAN output \r\nstage, making it easier to meet the required electrical and EMC \r\nperformance at a lower cost. In particular, it reduces the design\r\nconstraints on the internal circuitry compared to a transceiver \r\npowered entirely at 3.3V, reducing the component cost by a \r\nsignificant amount.\r\nAt the same time, the separate 3.3V VIO supply allows direct \r\ncommunication with the MCU without requiring additional level \r\nshifters."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 173.736 35.306 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "91e685a7-7d0c-4da6-b95f-37725ab03725")
	)
	(text ""
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 130.048 40.386 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "ab5c3a1e-0a7d-4e95-b669-4dad01b8c1b6")
	)
	(text "Debug Interface"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 359.664 21.59 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "b383b410-a616-41f0-9301-e4da629f47e2")
	)
	(text "The SWD programming connector is separated from the main one \nbecause it is not actually part of the final system, and it is \nused only during testbench debug phase, while a full debugger can \nbe useful. When the system will be relased, tehe Firmware can be \nupdated from a bootloader running on CAN bus, without the need \nfor a full debugger"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 339.598 28.956 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "b3b95247-ffcc-43fe-808d-6092fbe4d03d")
	)
	(text "Servo Potentiometer GND"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 289.814 84.074 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "c3bd4b94-7bdb-48fd-b594-9428982b775e")
	)
	(text "Interlock Start"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 370.078 73.914 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "cc2680d9-d7b0-47bf-9994-344c8116a33c")
	)
	(text "From LED datasheet the Typical Voltage drop at 5 mA is 2.95V.\nKnowing this, I can compute the series resistor:\n	R = (5V - V_forw_LED) / 5 mA = 410 Ohm --> (E12 series) R = 470 Ohm\n	P_loss = V * I = 5V * ((5V - 2.95V) / 470Ohm) = 21mW\n\nIf the Actual Forward Voltage of the LED is higher, it will simply light up with\nlower luminosity, so using the typical Forward Voltage value is ok (I don't need to \nbe precise on the actual current flowing in the LED, I just need it to light up)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 258.318 172.212 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "cfec153b-8c17-421e-b825-7cf908c0386b")
	)
	(text "Speed simulation"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 42.164 16.51 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "d23c7c67-62d6-4dba-ad8f-9b5954acc5b4")
	)
	(text "UART has a separated connector because it is not actually part of \nthe system, and is used only for debugf porpuses"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 339.852 51.308 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "d2977915-ab20-48da-8d6e-d284081e383d")
	)
	(text "Motor Driver Circuits"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 74.422 92.456 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "df18e6c9-0168-49d1-91d6-fdb79c3a5f60")
	)
	(text "TVS diode is used to improve the ESD strike resistance of the \nCAN tranciver. It allows to withstand automotive standard ISO \n10605 30kV stikes, in comparison to the 8kV strikes for which \nthe CAN tranciver is rated. \n\nTVS diode must be placed as close as possible the CAN connector \ntoprotect efficiently the CAN transceiver. A direct connection \nto wide GND plane is required to disspate efficiently energy \nfrom the ESD strike with a low impedance return path. \n\nThe TVS is bidirectional to proect against ESD strikes with both \npolarities. It has a 24V working voltage (V_RWM) to avoid \nconducting during normal operations and during possible \nsustained overvoltage conditions (1 CAN line is shorted to 12V \nMain Supply). \nThe clamping voltage (V_CL), the voltage reached during a strike \nis 33V, which is less than the maximum absolute rating of the \ntranciver CAN pins of 42V, ensuring that the strike won't \nactually damage the tranciver.\nThe capacitance of 31pF added on each CAN line is small, \nbasically not degrading the dynamic perfomance of the CAN.\n\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 207.264 85.09 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "f40e3162-479f-480c-9094-c29baf8583ac")
	)
	(text "Inductor is chosen following the rules in the comment below:\n    - DC Resistance <= 140 mOhm\n    - I_SAT >= 1.9 A (Design peak: 1.264 A, ~50% margin)\n    - I_RMS >= 1.25 A (Design RMS: 0.819 A, ~50% margin)\n    - Preferably shielded"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 180.848 158.242 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "fb91e79e-74f2-4b9d-841d-76e0f66067c5")
	)
	(text "VTRef"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 344.932 43.688 0)
